      "widgets_values": [
        "subject_definitions: <Picture 1> is the exact user's black fixed-gear bicycle, including its asymmetric wheel setup, pale-wrapped forward bullhorn, single silver bottle cage, straight fixed chain and specific frame geometry. <Picture 2> is the young Black British courier face identity; preserve recognizable face and hair.\nsummary: A single photorealistic live-action night departure shot outside a real London record-cutting workshop.\naction: The courier is stationary astride the bicycle, balanced, one shoe on the wet pavement and the other on a pedal. Both hands hold the bullhorn close to the stem. He looks along the street toward frame right, then makes one controlled push-off and rolls forward.\ncamera: Begin in a steady low side three-quarter master that clearly shows the whole bicycle, both wheels, both hands and the rider. The camera tracks smoothly with him to frame right for a few metres without crossing the axis or changing lens.\ncontinuity: Keep the exact bicycle from Picture 1 recognizable throughout the take: deep carbon front wheel remains at the front; normal tan-sidewall shallow rear wheel stays at the rear; white bar tape, silver bottle cage, single chainring and fixed rear cog remain stable. No derailleur, cassette, added hardware or extra wheels. The dark messenger bag is worn across the courier's body, never attached to the bicycle.\nsetting: Wet brick-and-steel workshop exterior at night; warm amber practical light through rain-speckled glass, cool-neutral fill, realistic reflections. No extra people or signage.\nstyle: Natural London street realism, authentic skin and fabric, true bicycle scale, physically credible wheel rotation and chain motion, restrained film grain. No cyberpunk, CGI gloss, neon wash, morphing, duplicate parts, malformed hands or invented logos. No generated music or dialogue."
